The holdout difference is effectively zero ($\Delta{=}{+}0.001$, within cross-seed noise), placing the transition boundary between the degradation regime ($\rho{=}0.2$, $\Delta{=}{+}0.046$) and the improvement regime ($\rho{=}0.4$, $\Delta{=}{-}0.020$) within $[0.3, 0.4]$.
At $\rho{=}0.3$, 28\% of micro-batches have $\bstar{=}0$ (clean, no intervention), compared to ${\sim}$2\% at $\rho{=}0.4$; the low intervention frequency explains why the method neither helps nor harms.
The progression across contamination levels---$\rho{=}0.2$ harmful ($+$0.046), $\rho{=}0.3$ neutral ($+$0.001), $\rho{=}0.4$ beneficial ($-$0.020)---shows a graceful transition rather than abrupt failure, consistent with the detector's signal-to-noise ratio gradually crossing the threshold needed for reliable $\aeff$ estimation.

